\begin_layout Standard
The main proposal being considered is GP, which uses evolutionary strategies
 to optimize formulas that can be interpreted by a human.
 In order to gain understanding, they thus offer a domain expert a good
 point of reference, but the solution path often deviates strongly from
 the strategy that an expert would choose.
 For some complex problems, fuzzy GP 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hein_fuzzy_gp"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 has shown that interpretable policies could be created.
 GP is an evolutionary ML approach that can automatically create programmes
 through evolutionary methods.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We argue that familiarity allows for a quicker and better understanding
 and provides some of the key requirements for good interpretability.
 We assume that these can be interpreted still deviate strongly from the
 preferred solution approach of a domain expert and require a complete new
 understanding of the solution, without the expert being able to use his
 already acquired knowledge.
 Assuming that the expert strategy already follows a correct approach, it
 therefore seems very helpful to be shown what would have to be changed
 in order to achieve the performance of a black box.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This technique of offline analysis could already be used manually by analysing
 the played games of AlphaGo, where new strategies emerged after finding
 structural differences to the known tactics.
 This process should be automated with our proposed principle of familiarity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We therefore propose to use GP to modify an expert's programme in such a
 way that he only has to comprehend the deviations in addition to his already
 known solution.
\end_layout

