\begin_layout Section
Contribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This thesis introduces the principle of familiarity, by means of which solutions
 are characterised as interpretable the less they deviate from the expert's
 solution.
 For this purpose, the complexity measure of classical GP, which is based
 on the number of nodes of the programming tree, is calculated as the number
 of necessary changes to the expert's programming tree.
 This is implemented with the help of the tree edit distance, which is already
 known as a problem in theoretical computer science.
 Thus, genetic programming would still try to achieve the same goal, but
 would be more bound to the heuristic given by the expert.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this work, the goal of GP is generated concretely as a regression problem
 on transition samples of a non-specific black box policy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Outline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The structure of this thesis is organized as follows.
 Chapter xx2provides background information covering the most important
 aspects of interpretability in the context of reinforcement learning.
 Chapter xx3 introduces the concept of familiarity and discribes the implementat
ion in the form of the tree edit distance as second order optimisation.
 Fixed nodes are further introduced as logical extension to some shortcomings
 of the edit distance.
 The approach is evaluated using symbolic regression on transition samples
 from neuropolicies covering both the mountaincar and the highly stochastic
 industrial benchmark.
 Chapter xx5 concludes the results and discusses several expansion possibilities
, especially enhancements for the edit distance and explorative regression.
\end_layout

