\begin_layout Section
x Reinforcement Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reinforcement learning (RL) is a subfield of Machine Learning, which deals
 with problems such as games or simulations, where the goal is obvious but
 a solution strategy must be sought.
 The goal is only implied in the form of an external reward, the sum of
 which an agent is supposed to maximise through interaction in an environment.
 To do so, the agent receives the current state (observations) of the environmen
t and can decide on an action, thus reaching a subsequent state and receiving
 a reward.
 The environment is formalized as Markov Decision Process (MDP), which is
 a stochastic form of determinism so that in each state, when performing
 an action, the same subsequent state is reached with a certain probability.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To learn a policy in such an environment therefore requires carefully weighting
 up exploration and exploitation to cope with the limited resources an agent
 has at his disposal.
 
\end_layout

