\begin_layout Section
x Genetic Programming 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp_Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Genetic programming (GP) is an evolutionary algorithm that can automatically
 create computer programs.
 It has become a well known approach for creating interpretable system controlin
g policies since it was first introduced by Koza.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Koza_GP_patent_1990"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It requires two essential things, one is the representation of a program
 as sequence of genes which allow for evolution strategies to be applied,
 the other one is a fitness function which rate candidates for the selection
 process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Representation as genome / Mutation Concepts 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The genetic encoding is mostly implemented as programming tree with the
 inner nodes being operators and the outer nodes either observations or
 constants.
 Regarding the concept of closure, which refers to the requirement that
 trees must always represent an interpretable expressions, type complications
 between boolean and numeric values must be adressed.
 The preferred Strongly Typed Genetic Programming (STGP), requires an explicit
 type conversion between number and truth values.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Operators for explicit conversion are, for example, comparison operators
 or if statements
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Strongly_typed_GP"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 Even though this is very implementation-heavy, the generated formulas seem
 to be much more practicable than automatic conversions.
 Evaluation safety poses a particular problem, as even type-correct expressions
 can be mathematically invalid.
 In the case of frequently occurring functions, the definition gaps for
 the very common division-function (divide by zero) returns the dummy value
 $1$.
 To avoid changing the essence of other operators too much, a tree can also
 simply be discarded in the case of rarely occurring errors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Evolving a population
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The evolution of a population is a metaheuristic optimization, which means
 that improvements occur stochastically.
 Unter der Annahme, dass das beste Programm die Kobination der besten Gene
 ist die Optimierung des Genpool von entscheidender Bedeutung.
 Durch eine Selektion und Reproduktion der besten Kandidaten für die nächste
 Generation vermehren sich gute Gene, durch Mutation und Rekombination soll
 die Selektion noch verbessert werden.
 Whatsoever, the evolutions rely on randomness and do not converge to an
 optimum.
\end_layout

