\begin_layout Subsection
Offline Fitness Function: Symbolic Regression xxx
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A symbolic regression is a common and important use case of GP for this
 approach, which is used to calculate how well a formula can explain data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Restricting the search space from complexity bloat 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp-complexity-depth-node-based"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to the high branching factor for solution candidates, the genotypic
 search space for trees must be strongly restricted.
 Thus, both the maximum tree depth and the number of nodes are usually limited.
 Formula components can also be additionally weighted to avoid complex mathemati
cal operators, for example the default weighting of the popular GP Framework
 EUREKA can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The amount of nodes is also suitable as second objective in a multi-objective
 optimization, where all pareto-efficient combinations to the first objective
 of fitness are saved.
 The resulting pareto front of candidates offer the opportunity for the
 user to weigh between the found candidates instead of saving only the best.
\end_layout

