\begin_layout Paragraph
Olymp-reproduction (Pareto reproduction)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similar to the Greek gods who came to earth to reproduce, the pareto candidates
 were also given the opportunity to intermingle with the current population.
 By chance, solutions already found and their genes could be completely
 lost.
 In order not to lose already found solutions permanently, the genes of
 the best candidates are to provide a very small current for backups of
 the best-ofs of the past.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

The olymp reproduction includes in particular the possibility for the origin
 tree to reproduce itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Latex tree visualisation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Although the visualisation of the candidates plays only a minor role, the
 form of representation should not be neglected as an interface for the
 developer.
 Therefore, in addition to the export options as a mathematical formula
 and as Python code, a visualisation of the tree was also implemented with
 the help of Latex.
 The main problem to be solved here is the arrangement of the nodes, which
 is made possible by the Forest package with the help of Latex.
 For very large trees, a tight-tree version is also created, in which branches
 with simple mathematical formulas (which do not involve fixed nodes) are
 bundled as individual nodes.
 This preserves the structure of the tree without inflating it with simple
 mathematical formulas.
 Nodes are combined if they are not fixed and if their expression is part
 of classical mathematical formulas.
 
