\begin_layout Section
Mathematical simplification 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
xxx simplification vs unification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following section, an extensive reduction of GP trees is presented,
 which is intended to improve the goal of interpretable formulae and the
 functioning of TED.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simplification of formulae deals with the problem of representing mathematic
al expressions by a minimum number of necessary expressions and thus as
 simply as possible.
 Less complex candidates are also preferred in GP, but complex branches
 can be important for the evolutionary process as it offers more starting
 points for improvements of new gene structures by random mutation or crossover.
 For most EAs, therefore, goal-directed simplification tends to take a subordina
te role and is mainly used for new Pareto entries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For familiarity-based GP, however, there are other ways to simplify a formula.
 By using symbolic mathematics, an expression, including all its variables,
 can be simplified and brought into a unified form.
 This includes, for example, the elimination of factors and the restructuring
 into a unique form, as can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"

\end_inset

.
 Thus, formula components are ordered uniformly, which counteracts the problem
 of TED, where commutativity and associativity are not taken into account.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $(a+b)=(b+a)$
\end_inset

 is mathematically equivalent, but the edit-distance between the two representat
ions is 2.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Float figure
placement h
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename img/inkscape/sympy_reduction_example.pdf

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

By simplifying the formula with Sympy, even the order of the elements is
 unique.
 Both starting formulas $(a+a)*2$ and $a+3 
\backslash
cdot a$ are thus always reduced to $a 
\backslash
cdot 4$.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Implementation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While simplifying formulas for more efficient evaluation can be done easily,
 creating a simpler branch is a much more complex problem.
 The extracted expression is simplified using symbolic mathematics, for
 which the Python library SymPy 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "10.7717/peerj-cs.103"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 was extended.
 The simplified expression is then evaluated as an abstract ayntax tree
 (AST), which can be transformed into a GP tree by recursively pattern matching
 the components to the available GP operators.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Due to its complexity, this process can lead to many special cases and in
 any case requires extensive adaptations, for example to avoid arity-changing
 or unwanted transformations as in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:sympy-custom-gp-operators"

\end_inset

 or to handle special cases of solutions, as can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:sympy-sonderfälle"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this work, the effects of simplified expert heuristics were not further
 processed, as this would have required a reinterpretation and weighting
 of various changes.
 Thus, the benefits of unified formulas could not be evaluated either.
 This approach was only used to improve the performance of the existing
 approach by trying to simplify Pareto entries.
\end_layout

