\begin_layout Subsection
Anomalies with node composition related complexity measures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of nodes poses some problems as a paradigm, even after weighting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important distinction has to be made between the inner and outer nodes
 of a tree.
 The inner nodes, which contain the operators, essentially characterise
 a programming tree by representing the computation that is performed., since
 the exact choice of leaf nodes are the logical prerequisite for its fitness
 and can therefore only be used in the appropriate places.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Operators 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Operators represent the heuristic of a tree and have the most debatable
 implications for explainability.
 Not only is the total set of operators used of interest, explicability
 depends above all on the structure and type of nesting.
 Examples of this are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{itemize}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
item Operators with a high branching factor results in more leaf nodes and
 also more complex models.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
item The amplification regarding the output of an operator can propagated
 disproportionately through a tree.
 E.g.
 the power function depends strongly on the exponent ($x+y$, $x
\backslash
cdot y$, $x^y$) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
item Specific requirements regarding the input of an operator.
 E.g.
 the exponent of a power $x^y$ should be a natural number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
item Recurring patterns in a tree can be recognised more easily
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{itemize}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph
Division into Chunks of Knowledge with the if-conditional
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The if-conditional exemplifies the contradiction between explainability
 measures and interpretability measures.
 Although the if-operator is the most complex function with 3 input parameters,
 it creates more structure than all other operators because its child trees
 are not combined but can be considered separately.
 Nevertheless, the subtrees must have a relation to each other in order
 to harmonize together.
 This combination creates encapsulated programs in a clear way that humans
 can understand more easily.
 It is always to be welcomed when these are contained in programs, but this
 should not be artificially forced, as they are not useful at every point.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, handling cases often are defined with the same 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
xxx The if statement is already penalized by its high branching factor,
 which should not be further supported by a higher weighting.
 
\end_layout

