\begin_layout Paragraph
Leaf nodes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The number of leaf nodes follows explicitly from the operator tree and can
 be the branching factor, i.e.
 the number of values to be allocated.
 A distinction should be made between observations and numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph
Numbers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Numbers are constant values which are rather irrelevant for understanding
 a formula.
 Even though they are essential for a program to function and optimizing
 their exact values is subject to an important part of the genetic evolution,
 besides some remarkable constants 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

(for example $
\backslash
pi$)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 or the general factor 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

($0.1, 10, 10.000$)
\end_layout

\end_inset

, they have no outstanding characteristic.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subparagraph
Observations 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "gp-Observations-role"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The observations used play a decisive role, but above all the set of unique
 operators is crucial.
 They are the nodes around which operators and constants are to be arranged
 in such a way that they map their values as well as possible to an output,
 which is, after all, the sole task of the GP process.
 Since not all available observations have to be used automatically, they
 mainly also represent the dimensionality of the input vector of the model
 that the solution tree represents.
 Dimensionality plays a crucial role for the mathematical space in which
 solutions are sought.
 High dimensional problems suffer from a number of problems also known as
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

curse of dimensionality
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and are therefore also more difficult to understand.
 For the developer, they represent in particular the starting point of his
 considerations, which is contrary to the genetic approach with the tree
 root as the main anchor point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Fallacy factors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The node depth should not be considered evaluating a node.
 Die Bedeutung eines Knotens ist meist nicht anhand seiner Position erkennbar,
 Kommutativität sowie Assoziativität vieler Rechenarten ist nur ein Beispiel.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Die maximale Baumtiefe wird häufig als Grenze für Bäume gewählt
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Die Arität von Operatoren muss nicht zusätzlich gewichtet werden, da durch
 den höheren Branchingfaktor automatisch die Komplexität steigt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Trimming the Tree of Edits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As already mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "gp-Observations-role"

\end_inset

, constants have a comparatively insignificant role for the interpretability
 or the essence of a formula anyway.
 Observations play an important role for the problem itself (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "gp-Observations-role"

\end_inset

), but also result from the pure operational tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, for normal complexity measures, the only function of evaluating
 leaf nodes is to penalise the branching factor of a tree without much additiona
l effort.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For distance-based methods, leaf nodes therefore represent a relatively
 increased factor.
 Especially constants have a strong impact, where an otherwise desired optimisat
ion of a value is evaluated as a full-fledged edit.
 This can not only prevent deliberate simplifications, but can even lead
 to the formula components around the variable being adjusted in order to
 be able to continue to use the constant unchanged.
 A simple solution would be, for example, to exclude constants when calculating
 the TED.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observations, on the other hand, have a much more important role, especially
 the amount of observations used.
 As the number of outputs are fixed, the number of necessary observations
 represents the dimensionality of the room of possible solutions, which
 is also known in mathematics as the curse of dimensionality when it is
 too large.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the quantity of operators used is more important than their number,
 the symmetrical distance between the quantities of observations used can
 also be extended and included in the calculation, for example as a sum
 or as a factor.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Note that this approach is based on the same paradigm edit-distance, but
 contradicts the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

identity of indiscernibles
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 condition of distance measurements.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 ; they should therefore not be ignored like the constants and can remain
 a normal part of the TED
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Weighted TED
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED offers many extension possibilities which can further address the
 nature of a change, including a weighting of nodes similar to the one used
 in classical GP which was mentioned in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this, the weighted mapping must be calculated.
 A subsequent weighting of the mapping of the regular TED does not make
 sense, since a later reweighting can lead to ambiguous results that do
 not correspond to the minimum.
 This mainly concerns the handling of a changed node.
 The weighted TED was imlemented, but opens many questions on how to deal
 with the effect and the complexity of altered nodes in the origin tree.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Implementations might be based on the following ideas:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Ignoring altered nodes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Division of all operators into classes, where the cost for changing within
 a class is weakened.
 A potential class might be the operator families (e.g.
 angle-based operators.
 This allows adaptions in konstant nodes and also a distinction between
 basic operator types.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Even more specific options that were implemented but not evaluated in order
 not to mix too many paradigms:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
With if-statements, the edit distance of the child branches is calculated
 separately and only the largest factor is added.
 This procedure is intended to promote the use of if statements, which have
 a very positive influence on the interpretability of a formula due to their
 structuring influence, but which greatly increase the TED due to the high
 arity of 3 and the resulting branching factor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use of several known origins in one run and counting the minimum TED for
 each tree.
 This way, several understandable approaches could be compared directly
 for their extensibility or even incomplete solutions could be introduced
 separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Unifying and ordering trees, for example with sympify as described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:sympy-ordered-expression"

\end_inset

, before calculating the TED.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Similar to fixed nodes (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset

), giving nodes of the core structure a separate weighting so that changes
 are possible but very costly.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
